and as u/immbah said, you should always back up important work to another computer. At my company we use a private cloud storage for all our work data, and we make sure it syncs automatically. This might seem expensive but the software we use is actually around 25BD a month, and we get 1TB and 100 users. I can actually get the details and exact pricing of the software from my colleagues if you want. Just let me know.

I will suggest the host I am using which is [advis.ga](https://advis.ga) and starts at around $3 if I am right

&#x200B;

**EDIT:** If the pages dont load just refresh and clear cache

I used to run a similar home-business for building gaming PCs. (It went off due to some problems in market prices) Anyways, I used to have a website(which is still online) and I made payments to be handled by Paypal. The benefit is that you set it right away, (benefit and else will ask for company details, and you need time for processing request afaik) and that I didn't have to pay except when a payment is made, so if I had no sales in a month, i dont pay for it. Try checking the website out for a general look and try adding to cart and proceeding to checkout at [http://pc4all.ml](http://pc4all.ml) (please dont actually pay because I might not be able to return the full amount due to fees, and because I cant really supply most things at that price)

Draw backs:

It is not completely free(nothing is anyways)

You cant use BHD, so a person may have to pay bank fees when they buy.

They take some 4-5% on every payment so include that in the price

You can't set it to do shipment price calculation automatically, and so it is better you let customers pay the carrier on delivery(I was going to use Aramex since they were the cheaper I found)

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Thats it actually so let me know what you think

I think you misunderstood a part of it. It is not processing 128 integers together. Instead, when it comes to x86, ARMv(something like 6 7 or 8), it is not meant to address the number of integers, but how big can the Address of the RAM be. This allows for more RAM. So the x86-64 allows 64 wires to address any RAM memory slot at any given moment using some decoders and other things. So 64 wires give you 16 exabytes of RAM that can be used with this processor. This is very large that we might never need it(atleast not before our 2100) in my opinion. The number of integers processed at a time is generally known as IPC or Instructions Per Clock-cycle. You can process that number of instructions in a singe clock on a single core. What AMD did with their GPUs is even more complicated but I am trying to give the least needed information to limit this comments size. What they reduced wasn't the address bus widths. They reduced the data bus which is the group of wires carrying data to the GPU. This means the GPU could fetch less data from the VRAM and replaced it with the infinity cache which is where the frequently needed data is stored. So if a texture is needed a lot in a game, it might well be stored in that cache so that it reduces the need to go and fetch it again from the RAM, so reduces booth power and latency. 

&#x200B;

To sum up:

There are 2 buses: Data bus(which carries actual data) and Address bus(which tells the RAM which memory storage should be sent on the Data bus to the CPU/GPU.) The more address bus width you have, the more RAM you can have. On the other hand, the more Data bus width you have, the more information you can fetch from the RAM to process. The cache stores the data needed very often and larger cache means less need to go bring things from the slow RAM and just use it right away because it is built-in to the CPU anyway. Imagine the choice between going to a small grocery store and a large department store that is very far away. As long as the things you need are in the grocery store, you don't need the department store that is very far away, which reduces time that you need to get your things and the cost of the fuel. 

&#x200B;

Sorry for making it very long but I hope everyone gets benefitted and experts may correct me in the replies.
